# sentry-micro as an ESP-IDF component. Option names match the Zephyr module's
# (zephyr/Kconfig) wherever the option means the same thing, so a setting carries across.

menu "sentry-micro"

    config SENTRY_MICRO_SESSIONS
        bool "Sessions, for release health"
        default y
        help
            Count each boot as one session in Sentry's release health. The
            session opens at sentry_init() and is closed by the next boot from
            its reset reason: a panic, a watchdog or a brownout ends it as
            crashed, anything else as exited. Updates are sent by
            sentry_flush() once the device knows the date, because Sentry
            drops a session that is not dated; a build with no clock source
            never sends them. Needs a release in sentry_options_t.

            The record is kept beside the log ring in RTC memory, which
            survives a reset but not a power cycle. Logs and metrics are
            switched with the SENTRY_MICRO_LOGS_ENABLED and
            SENTRY_MICRO_METRICS_ENABLED defines rather than here.

    config SENTRY_MICRO_TRANSPORT_WIFI
        bool "WiFi transport: HTTPS straight to ingest"
        default y
        help
            POST envelopes to Sentry ingest over HTTPS through esp_http_client
            and ESP-TLS, with no host in between. Register it with
            sentry_wifi_transport_init() from
            <transport/esp-idf/sentry_transport_wifi.h>.

            Despite the name it is not tied to the radio: it sends over the
            interface lwIP routes through and is available whenever the
            default interface has an IPv4 address. The application owns the
            network: it brings WiFi (or Ethernet) up, and examples/idf_basic
            shows how.

            Never call it from a panic handler: the HTTP client and the TLS
            handshake allocate from the heap.

    config SENTRY_MICRO_WIFI_TLS_VERIFY
        bool "Verify ingest's TLS certificate"
        default y
        depends on SENTRY_MICRO_TRANSPORT_WIFI
        help
            Check that the server's certificate chains to a trusted root and
            names the ingest host. The root is DigiCert Global Root G2,
            bundled with sentry-micro, which Sentry's ingest certificate
            chains to; an application can pass its own root to
            sentry_wifi_transport_init() instead. Certificate dates are not
            checked unless MBEDTLS_HAVE_TIME_DATE is set, so this works before
            the clock is.

            The bundled root expires on 2038-01-15, and stops matching sooner
            if Sentry moves ingest to a certificate from another root. Either
            way every handshake then fails until the firmware carries a root
            that matches, so a fleet that cannot be updated should consider
            passing its own, or SENTRY_MICRO_WIFI_TLS_CRT_BUNDLE.

            Turn it off only to debug a handshake, or for an endpoint whose
            certificate cannot be verified. ESP-TLS then needs ESP_TLS_INSECURE
            and ESP_TLS_SKIP_SERVER_CERT_VERIFY as well, and the build stops
            until they are set. The connection is encrypted but not
            authenticated: anything on the path can pose as ingest and read
            the DSN key from the auth header. The transport logs a warning on
            its first send.

    config SENTRY_MICRO_WIFI_TLS_CRT_BUNDLE
        bool "Trust ESP-IDF's certificate bundle instead of the bundled root"
        default n
        depends on SENTRY_MICRO_WIFI_TLS_VERIFY && MBEDTLS_CERTIFICATE_BUNDLE
        help
            Verify ingest against ESP-IDF's bundle of common root certificates
            (esp_crt_bundle_attach) rather than the one DigiCert root
            sentry-micro carries. The bundle keeps working if Sentry changes
            certificate authority, at a cost of tens of KB of flash, set by
            the MBEDTLS_DEFAULT_CERTIFICATE_BUNDLE choice. A CA passed to
            sentry_wifi_transport_init() still takes precedence.

    config SENTRY_MICRO_WIFI_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "WiFi transport network timeout, in ms"
        default 10000
        range 1000 120000
        depends on SENTRY_MICRO_TRANSPORT_WIFI
        help
            esp_http_client's network timeout, applied to each step of a
            send: the connect and TLS handshake, each write of the request,
            and the wait for the response headers. Name resolution is bounded
            by lwIP instead.

endmenu
